[Comments]
Count=45
1=Of important note, the November rain crust is starting to break down in shallow snow areas and the strong 
2=temperature gradient is facetting the snow pack. With the crust breaking down, the weak depth hoar below it, 
3=and now a buried surface hoar layer near the surface, will be the weaknesses to watch over the long term. 
4=Additional loading in the form of skiers and/or new snow on shallow trigger locations linked to a slab could 
5=cause these weaknesses to potentially fail to ground given the right slope.
6=
7=STABILITY TESTS SUMMARY FOR PARKERS WEATHER PLOT:
8=1. compression test hard 23 @ 12cm in depth hoar 3mm sudden collapse.
9=2. shovel test hard @ 12cm depth hoar 3mm column break.
10=-both results below November rain crust.
11=
12=SNOWPACK 2 WEEK SUMMARY (last profile Dec 18, 2004, see past profiles)
13=Depth range over terrain: Variability is the key word. Things feel like it was 1 month ago with 2m (wind
14=pocket) to no snow (wind exposed feature) in the space of a few meters. The only storm since Dec 18 was Dec 
15=19 with 10 cm of new warm snow. It has generally bonded well.
16=Stability tests: Depending where one is on the landscape and the characteristic of your snowpack, stability 
17=tests are ranging from Hard to Easy. Generally, tests are trending towards the Moderate and Hard range failing 
18=on the persistant weaknesses of the November rain crust and basal depth hoar. In many locations, a hard 
19=supportive surface slab has developed which can release in the Moderate to Easy range. However, it is 
20=bridging the weak facets below at this point quite well.
21=Structure: The Dec 19 storm fell with warm temperatures and rained to 2000m. Since then, we have only 
22=received small amounts of snow. Warm temperatures continued after this storm firming this snow. We observed 
23=mainly rounds and mixed forms in the top layers. The recent cold temperatures are now changing the entire 
24=snowpack to facets with well developed depth hoar at the bottom. Mixed forms can still be found in the upper 
25=layers but are rapidly changing to facets. The November rain crust is decomposing in shallow snowpack zones 
26=where less than 80cm. Where more than 80cm, the november rain crust can still be found intact.
27=Snow temperatures: Frigid temperatures are promoting facetting throughout the snowpack. 
28=
29=WEATHER 2 WEEK SUMMARY (since dec 18)
30=HN/HST: Approximately 10cm fell on Dec 19. Since then, only 10 cms of new have arrived in the form of 1 or 2 
31=a day. We are hovering around 80cm height of snow at the weather plot. This height is shrinking with 
32=settlement.
33=Penetration: A firm surface wind slab has developed throughout the forecast area. Penetration is only a few 
34=cm's. Where snow is less consolidated and protected from the wind in the trees, ski penetration can be 30cm 
35=or boot penetration to ground. 
36=
37=AVALANCHE OBSERVATIONS: (since Dec 18: 
38=After the Dec 19 storm, some size 1 sluffing was observed on steep rock slabs but overall natural avalanche 
39=activity was very limited. Currently, we are starting to see a few more size 1 point releases out of steep rocks at 
40=all aspects at about 2200m elevation. They are travelling only to the upper runout zone. This is likely a shallow 
41=snowpack condition that has facetted out and lost its internal cohesion. Generally speaking, the warm storm 
42=snow followed by frigid temperatures have 'tightened' everything in; however, many weak trigger zones still 
43=exist out there.  
